WEBVTT

00:00:06.636 --> 00:00:10.015
We will load a small dictionary one line at a time,

00:00:10.015 --> 00:00:13.394
then ask whether a fragment begins any stored word.

00:00:13.394 --> 00:00:16.441
This supports a Ghost turn without requiring a

00:00:16.441 --> 00:00:17.368
complete word.

00:00:17.368 --> 00:00:20.747
The familiar writeString call prepares our practice

00:00:20.747 --> 00:00:22.536
file before reading begins.

00:01:03.518 --> 00:01:06.664
A prefix is a sequence at the start of a String.

00:01:06.664 --> 00:01:09.875
The hasPrefix method checks each stored word with

00:01:09.875 --> 00:01:12.890
startsWith and returns as soon as one matches.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:15.314
If no word matches, it returns false.

00:01:15.314 --> 00:01:18.591
A contains check would instead look for a complete

00:01:18.591 --> 00:01:19.443
stored value.

00:01:19.748 --> 00:01:22.960
A character stream supplies decoded text characters

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:23.590
over time.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:26.551
BufferedReader holds groups in memory to reduce

00:01:26.551 --> 00:01:28.503
repeated small file operations.

00:01:28.503 --> 00:01:31.653
readLine returns the next line without its ending.

00:01:31.653 --> 00:01:34.046
A blank line produces an empty String.

00:01:34.046 --> 00:01:37.133
Null marks end of file when no characters remain.

00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:40.408
The loop reads before its condition and again at the

00:01:40.408 --> 00:01:40.660
end.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.865
The length check skips blank lines.

00:01:42.865 --> 00:01:46.014
HashSet keeps unique words, and try with resources

00:01:46.014 --> 00:01:47.148
closes the reader.

00:01:47.243 --> 00:01:50.362
Predict the word count and both prefix results.

00:01:50.362 --> 00:01:53.613
Trace the blank line and repeated word carefully.

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:54.343
Pause here.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:02.183
The dictionary contains three unique words.

00:02:02.183 --> 00:02:05.743
The repeated cat adds no second entry, and the blank

00:02:05.743 --> 00:02:06.975
line adds nothing.

00:02:06.975 --> 00:02:10.329
The prefix c a matches the start of cat and cart.

00:02:10.329 --> 00:02:12.450
No stored word starts with z z.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:15.873
A prefix can match without being a complete stored

00:02:15.873 --> 00:02:16.215
word.

00:02:16.564 --> 00:02:20.299
Change only the first queried prefix from c a to c a t

00:02:20.299 --> 00:02:20.438
s.

00:02:20.438 --> 00:02:23.619
Predict that result and whether the word count

00:02:23.619 --> 00:02:25.280
changes, then run again.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.876
What evidence separates a prefix query from changing

00:02:28.876 --> 00:02:29.914
the dictionary?
